                <section class="content-section" id="erd">
                    <h2>Entity Relationship Diagram</h2>
                    <p>
                        The core challenge of SAM is that IT data (Installations) and Procurement data (Entitlements) speak different languages. The <strong>Software Model</strong> acts as the central translator linking these two worlds.
                    </p>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">SAM Core Tables ERD</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        erDiagram
                            cmdb_ci_computer ||--o{ cmdb_sam_sw_install : "Hosts"
                            cmdb_sam_sw_discovery_model ||--|{ cmdb_sam_sw_install : "Normalizes"
                            cmdb_software_product_model ||--|{ cmdb_sam_sw_discovery_model : "Maps to"
                            cmdb_software_product_model ||--|{ alm_license : "Licensed by"
                            sys_user ||--o{ alm_entitlement_user : "Assigned"
                            alm_license ||--o{ alm_entitlement_user : "Allocated to"
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="core-tables">
                    <h2>Core Table Breakdown</h2>
                    
                    <h3>1. Software Installation (<code>cmdb_sam_sw_install</code>)</h3>
                    <p><strong>The What and Where.</strong> This table holds the raw data discovered on a device. It links directly to a hardware CI (like a Windows Server) and contains the messy publisher/product strings found in the registry.</p>
                    
                    <h3>2. Discovery Model (<code>cmdb_sam_sw_discovery_model</code>)</h3>
                    <p><strong>The Normalized Concept.</strong> Thousands of identical <code>cmdb_sam_sw_install</code> records (e.g., all installations of Adobe Acrobat DC 2020) point to a single Discovery Model. This is the output of the Normalization engine.</p>
                    
                    <h3>3. Software Model (<code>cmdb_software_product_model</code>)</h3>
                    <p><strong>The Business Concept.</strong> This is the specific product you actually care about managing from a business perspective. It connects the normalized technical data (Discovery Models) to your financial purchases (Entitlements). It also tracks product lifecycles like End of Support (EOS).</p>
                    
                    <h3>4. Entitlement (<code>alm_license</code>)</h3>
                    <p><strong>The Purchase.</strong> This table represents your legal right to use a software product. It dictates the License Metric (e.g., Per User, Per Core), the number of rights purchased, and the cost. It points directly to a Software Model.</p>

                    <h3>5. Software Allocation (<code>alm_entitlement_user</code> or <code>alm_entitlement_asset</code>)</h3>
                    <p><strong>The Assignment.</strong> When you reserve a license right for a specific person or server, an allocation record is created linking the User/Asset to the Entitlement. This ensures rights aren't double-counted.</p>
                </section>
        
